{"name":"Navajo White","hex":"#ffdead","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Navajo white","blocks":[{"text":"Navajo White is an orangish white color, or pastel yellow orange, and derives its name from its similarity to the background color of the Navajo Nation flag. The name \"Navajo White\" is usually only used when referring to paint. Despite its name, the color is not a shade of white, but rather of yellow or of orange."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navajo_white","wikidata":"Q1406286"},{"title":"X11 color names","blocks":[{"text":"In computing, on the X Window System, X11 color names are represented in a simple text file, which maps certain strings to RGB color values. It was traditionally shipped with every X11 installation, hence the name, and is usually located in <X11root>/lib/X11/rgb.txt. The web colors list is descended from it but differs for certain color names."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X11_color_names","wikidata":"Q376249"},{"title":"Navajo","blocks":[{"text":"The Navajo are an Indigenous People of the Southwestern United States. The Navajo term for themselves is Diné. Their language is Navajo, a Southern Athabaskan language. The states with the largest Diné populations are Arizona (140,263) and New Mexico (108,305). More than three-quarters of the Diné population resides in these two states."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Manuelito.jpg/330px-Manuelito.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navajo","wikidata":"Q108266"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Shades_of_red.png/500px-Shades_of_red.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Shades_of_red.png/500px-Shades_of_red.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AShades_of_red.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/19th_century_knowledge_indian_lore_navaho_house.jpg/500px-19th_century_knowledge_indian_lore_navaho_house.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/19th_century_knowledge_indian_lore_navaho_house.jpg/500px-19th_century_knowledge_indian_lore_navaho_house.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 19th-century hogan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A19th_century_knowledge_indian_lore_navaho_house.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c6/Navaho_spinning_and_weaving_page_928.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c6/Navaho_spinning_and_weaving_page_928.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Navajo spinning and weaving on vertical loom","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANavaho_spinning_and_weaving_page_928.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Eingeborene_des_n%C3%B6rdlichen_Neu-Mexiko_1861.jpg/500px-Eingeborene_des_n%C3%B6rdlichen_Neu-Mexiko_1861.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Eingeborene_des_n%C3%B6rdlichen_Neu-Mexiko_1861.jpg/500px-Eingeborene_des_n%C3%B6rdlichen_Neu-Mexiko_1861.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"The Indigenous People of Northern New Mexico,\" by Balduin Möllhausen, 1861","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEingeborene_des_n%C3%B6rdlichen_Neu-Mexiko_1861.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Navajo_woman_%26_child.jpg/500px-Navajo_woman_%26_child.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Navajo_woman_%26_child.jpg/1280px-Navajo_woman_%26_child.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Navajo_woman_%26_child.jpg/500px-Navajo_woman_%26_child.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Navajo woman and child, circa 1880–1910","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANavajo_woman_%26_child.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Navajo_Woman_and_Infant%2C_Canyon_de_Chelle%2C_Arizona_%28Canyon_de_Chelly_National_Monument%29%2C_1933_-_1942_-_NARA_-_519947.jp